"efficient-sumcheck/CHANGELOG.md at main · compsec-epfl/efficient-sumcheck · GitHub (https://github.com/compsec-epfl/efficient-sumcheck/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md)\nciteturn2view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://github.com/compsec-epfl/efficient-sumcheck/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 264\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn2view1 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://github.com/compsec-epfl/efficient-sumcheck/blob/main/LICENSE-MIT\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nefficient-sumcheck/docs/design.md at main · compsec-epfl/efficient-sumcheck · GitHub (https://github.com/compsec-epfl/efficient-sumcheck/blob/main/docs/design.md)\nciteturn2view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn1view1\",\"pattern\":\"MSB\"}); Total lines: 699\nL264:      * MSB (half-split): fold the topmost variable. Pairs `(v[k], v[k+L/2])`. Best for in-memory and random-access-streaming workloads.\nL265:      * LSB (pair-split): fold the bottommost variable. Pairs `(v[2k], v[2k+1])`. Best for sequential-streaming workloads where data arrives incrementally.\nL266: \nL267: These choices are orthogonal: you can use blendy with MSB ordering (large witness on SSD) or blendy with LSB ordering (Jolt CPU trace).\nL268: ### Streaming taxonomy\nL269: \nL270: The choice of variable ordering depends on how data is available:\nL271: \nL272: Scenario  | Data availability  | Access  | Best ordering  | Strategy\nL273: --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL274: In-memory  | Full table in RAM  | Random  | MSB  | Time\nL275: Random-access stream  | Exists on disk, too big for RAM  | Seekable  | MSB  | Blendy\nL276: Sequential stream  | Generated incrementally  | Forward-only  | LSB  | Blendy\nL277: Random-access streaming (e.g., large witness mmap'd from SSD): the data exists but doesn't fit in RAM. MSB ordering has better cache behavior because it reads two contiguous half-table regions; the blendy working set fits in cache while the full table is paged in as needed.\nL278: Sequential streaming (e.g., Jolt CPU trace): evaluations are computed on-the-fly and arrive in index order (0, 1, 2, ...). LSB ordering is optimal because adjacent pairs `(f[2k], f[2k+1])` are immediately available — the prover can begin folding before the full table exists.\nL279: \nL280: In both streaming cases, blendy is used because the full table doesn't fit in the working set. Blendy is the space strategy; MSB vs LSB is the traversal order. They are independent.\nL281: ### Blendy stage scheduling\nL282: \nL283: Standard blendy (CFFZ24) partitions v variables into stages of fixed size k, recomputing the partial-sum table once per stage. The optimal k depends on the ratio of cache sizes to element size.\nL284: \nL285: Jolt's `HalfSplitSchedule` (based on BCFFMMZ25, eprint 2025/1473) takes a different approach: cost-model-driven, non-uniform window sizes.\nL286: For a degree-d sumcheck, the cost of processing a window of w variables starting at round i is `(d+1)^w / 2^(w+i) * T` where T is the trace length. Setting cost ~ 1 gives optimal window size:\nL287: \nL288:     `w(i) = round(ratio * i)    where ratio = ln(2) / ln((d+1)/2)\nL289:     `\nL290: \nL291: This produces growing windows: early rounds use small windows (the hypercube is large), later rounds use larger windows (the residual sum is small). For degree 2, the windows grow as 1, 2, 5, 14, ...\nL292: Degree  | Ratio  | Example window sequence\nL293: --- | --- | ---\nL294: 2  | 1.71  | 1, 2, 5, 14, ...\nL295: 3  | 1.00  | 1, 1, 2, 3, 4, ...\nL296: 4  | 0.76  | 1, 1, 1, 2, 2, 3, ...\nL297: \nL298: The schedule is parameterized by a `StreamingSchedule` trait, not a fixed constant:\nL299: \nL300:     pub trait StreamingSchedule {\nL301:         fn num_rounds(&self) -> usize;\nL302:         fn switch_over_point(&self) -> usize;\nL303:         fn is_window_start(&self, round: usize) -> bool;\nL304:         fn num_unbound_vars(&self, round: usize) -> usize;\nL305:     }\nL306: This allows tuning per deployment target and polynomial degree.\nL307: ### Strategies table\nL308: \nL309: Per Thaler §4.4.3 and prior work (CTY11, VSBW13, CFFZ24, BCFFMMZ25):\nL310: \nL311: Strategy  | Space  | Total time  | Input  | Ordering  | Ref\nL312: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL313: Time  | O(2^v)  | O(2^v)  | Vec<F>  | MSB  | VSBW13\nL314: Blendy  | O(2^k)  | O(2^v)  | Stream<F>  | MSB or LSB  | CFFZ24, BCFFMMZ25\nL315: Space  | O(v)  | O(v * 2^v)  | Stream<F>  | MSB or LSB  | CTY11\nL316: \nL317: All implement `SumcheckProver<F>`. The difference is internal: how `round()` computes the polynomial from the data.\nL318: ### Construction\nL319: \nL320:     // In-memory (MSB, time strategy).\nL321:     impl<F: SumcheckField> MultilinearProver<F> {\nL322:         pub fn new(evals: Vec<F>) -> Self;\nL323:     }\nL324: \nL325:     // Streaming (LSB or MSB, blendy strategy).\nL326:     impl<F: SumcheckField> StreamingMultilinearProver<F> {\nL327:         /// Random-access stream, MSB ordering. Best for mmap'd data.\nL328:         pub fn new_msb<S: Stream<F>>(stream: S, k: usize) -> Self;\nL329: \nL330:         /// Sequential stream, LSB ordering. Best for incremental data (Jolt).\nL331:         pub fn new_lsb<S: Stream<F>>(stream: S, k: usize) -> Self;\nL332:     }\nL333: The `Stream` trait (already in `src/streams/`) provides random access to evaluations without requiring the full table in memory.\nL334: ## 5. Three polynomial shapes\nL335: Each polynomial shape (multilinear, product, general) has its own prover type for each strategy, but all implement `SumcheckProver<F>`:\nL336: \nL337:     /// g = f_tilde, degree 1. Prover folds evals via Lemma 4.3.\nL338:     pub struct MultilinearProver<F> { evals: Vec<F> }\nL339: \nL340:     /// g = f_tilde * g_tilde, degree 2. Prover folds both vectors.\nL341:     pub struct InnerProductProver<F> { a: Vec<F>, b: Vec<F> }\nL342: \nL343:     /// g = user-defined, degree d. Wraps a RoundPolyEvaluator.\nL344:     pub struct CoefficientProver<F, E: RoundPolyEvaluator<F>> { ... }\nL345: ### The time prover's round() (Lemma 4.3 and 4.5)\nL346: \nL347: For a multilinear f over {0,1}^v with evaluations in array A:\nL348: \nL349:     `A[x'] = r_1 * A[1, x'] + (1 - r_1) * A[0, x']\nL350:     `\nL351: \nL352: This is the fold operation (Lemma 4.3, equation 4.13). After folding, the array has half the entries, and the prover can compute the next round polynomial from the folded array.\nL353: For a product of k multilinears (Lemma 4.5), the same fold is applied to each factor independently, and the round polynomial is computed from the folded factors.\nL354: ## 6. The fold primitive\nL355: \nL356: The fold operation (Thaler Lemma 4.3, equation 4.13) is the computational core of the time prover:\nL357: \nL358:     /// Half-split (MSB) fold: new[k] = v[k] + weight * (v[k + L/2] - v[k]).\nL359:     ///\nL360:     /// Implicit zero padding for non-power-of-two inputs.\nL361:     /// SIMD-accelerated for Goldilocks on NEON and AVX-512 IFMA.\nL362:     pub fn fold<F: Field>(values: &mut Vec<F>, weight: F);\nL363: The fold is exposed as a standalone public function because callers (e.g., WHIR's `multilinear_fold`) need it independently of the full sumcheck protocol.\nL364: ### Layout: half-split (MSB)\nL365: \nL366: We fold the top-most variable each round: `v[0..L/2]` vs `v[L/2..L]`. This is the MSB (most-significant-bit-first) convention. It matches Thaler's equation 4.13 directly:\nL367: \nL368:     `p(x_1, ..., x_l) = x_1 * p(1, x_2, ..., x_l) + (1 - x_1) * p(0, x_2, ..., x_l)\nL369:     `\nL370: \nL371: where `p(1, ...)` is the upper half and `p(0, ...)` is the lower half.\nL439: The runner and the verifier are both written once against this format; every concrete prover (multilinear, inner-product, GKR, eq-factored, coefficient of any degree, MSB or LSB) produces it.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nReleases · ingonyama-zk/icicle · GitHub (https://github.com/ingonyama-zk/icicle/releases)\nciteturn2view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn1view3\",\"pattern\":\"v4\"}); Total lines: 877\nL136: ## Release list\nL137: \nL138:   * cite71†Release v4.0.0 L139:   * cite72†Release v3.9.2 L140:   * cite73†Release v3.9.1 L141:   * cite74†Release v3.9.0 L142:   * cite75†Release v3.8.0 L143:   * cite76†Release v3.7.1 L144:   * cite77†Release v3.7.0 L145:   * cite78†Release v3.6.0 L146:   * cite79†Release v3.5.0 L147:   * cite80†Release v3.4.0 L148: \nL149: Jump to release\nL150: \nL151: ## Release v4.0.0\nL152: \nL153: cite81†Release v4.0.0 L154: \nL155: cite82†Latest L156: \nL157: Compare\nL158: \nL159: # Choose a tag to compare\nL160: \nL161: ## Sorry, something went wrong.\nL162: \nL163: Filter\nL164: \nL165: Loading [Input]\nL166: \nL167: ## Sorry, something went wrong.\nL168: ### Uh oh!\nL169: \nL170: There was an error while loading. Please reload this page.\nL171: \nL172: ## No results found\nL173: \nL174: cite70†View all tags L175: \nL176: cite83†Image: @github-actions†avatars.githubusercontent.com cite84†github-actions released this 11 Jul 05:36\nL177: \nL178: cite85†v4.0.0 L179: \nL180: cite86†`6c77775` L181: \nL182: # ICICLE v4.0.0 Release Notes\nL183: ## 🎉 Major Release: ICICLE v4.0.0\nL184: \nL185: We're excited to announce the release of ICICLE v4.0.0! This major version introduces significant improvements to the API design, making it more intuitive, object-oriented, and developer-friendly across all supported languages.\nL186: ## 🚀 Key Highlights\nL187:   * Object-Oriented Field API: Complete redesign of field operations from static methods to instance methods\nL188:   * Method Chaining Support: Write more concise and expressive code with method chaining\nL189:   * Improved Rust API: Better trait design with standard operator overloading\nL190:   * Enhanced Program API: Reorganized Program module with better type safety\nL191:   * Backward Compatibility: Comprehensive migration guides for v3 to v4 transition\nL192:   * Post Quantum: Implemented a NIST FIPS203 (ML-KEM) protocol on CUDA, fully open-source\nL193:   * Lattice Based Cryptography: Add support for Lattice based cryptography, with integer ring support and a full LaBRADOR prover (soon announced)\nL194: ## 🔄 Breaking Changes\nL195: \nL196: ### C++ API Changes\nL197: \nL198: #### Field Arithmetic Operations\nL199: \nL200: All field operations have been converted from static methods to instance methods\nL201: Check the cite87†migration page†dev.ingonyama.com for more details\nL202: \nL203: ### Rust API Changes\nL204: \nL205: #### Arithmetic Trait Redesign\nL206: \nL207: The `FieldImpl` trait has been deprecated and split into focused, orthogonal traits\nL209:   * Negacyclic NTT for polynomial rings by cite88†@yshekel in cite89†#930 L210:   * Update README.md by cite90†@Shalmoni in cite91†#931 L211:   * Fixed devdocs link licensing by cite90†@Shalmoni in cite92†#933 L250:   * ICICLE V4 by cite121†@ChickenLover , cite102†@LeonHibnik in cite141†#982 L251:   * [Feat] matmul + transpose fusion by cite108†@lnm-at-work in cite142†#1000 L252:   * [EXAMPLE] Add Rust example showcasing icicle-lattice APIs by cite88†@yshekel in cite143†#965 L253:   * [FIX] compilation for arm by cite88†@yshekel in cite144†#1001 L254:   * Some more changes to devdocs by cite90†@Shalmoni in cite145†#981 L255:   * ICICLE V4 documentation update by cite102†@LeonHibnik in cite146†#999 L256:   * [FIX] Enable and fix examples by cite93†@jeremyfelder in cite147†#972 ----------------------------------------------------------------------